  "2d720ca4-1ed0-45f3-9ee1-131c19701cb4": {
   "id": "2d720ca4-1ed0-45f3-9ee1-131c19701cb4",
   "message": {
    "id": "2d720ca4-1ed0-45f3-9ee1-131c19701cb4",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1764396617.159,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "Pumping usually means the duck is too deep and the release too slow for a bass that's sustaining. A cleaner setup:\n\n**Option 1: Fruity Limiter sidechain (tuned)**\n1. Route kick → 808's mixer track as sidechain (right-click the arrow on the 808 track from the kick track → \"Sidechain to this track\").\n2. On the 808 track: Fruity Limiter → COMP mode → set SIDECHAIN to the kick's input.\n3. Threshold so you get ~3–6 dB gain reduction (not 15).\n4. Ratio 4:1–10:1, **attack 0–1 ms, release 40–80 ms** (short, so the 808 comes back right after the kick transient).\n\n**Option 2: Volume shaping (cleaner for 808s)**\nUse Fruity Peak Controller on the kick → link to the 808 track's volume (inverted), or use a ducking plugin (e.g., a volume-shaper like LFOTool-style or the free Kickstart-type) triggered per kick.\n\n**Option 3: Fix it arrangement-side**\n- Don't let the kick and 808 hit at the same time unless intended; offset the 808 by a 1/32 or start it after the kick.\n- Pick a kick with a short tail or high-pass the kick's sub below ~40 Hz and let the 808 own the sub.\n- Tune the kick to the 808's key (or the root).\n\nUsually option 1 tuned + option 3 tuning gets rid of the mud."
     ]
    },
